  "method": "SoQL sum of NUMBER_OF_PERSONS_KILLED grouped by year of CRASH_DATE in h9gi-nx95, 2013-2025. DOT's official 2025 and 2024 totals and 2025 borough counts were read from DOT's year-end press release and checked to sum to the total. Peers: sum of FATALS in FARS accident.csv by STATE and COUNTY, divided by county population, times 100,000.",
  "caveats": [
    "DOT's official count is lower than the NYPD collision data, not higher: DOT reported 205 traffic deaths in 2025 and 253 in 2024; the NYPD collision data sum to 229 and 268. DOT publishes its count in press releases rather than as an annual data series, so the NYPD series is used here and DOT is the cross-check. Source: https://www.nyc.gov/html/dot/html/pr2026/traffic-deaths-reach-all-time-low.shtml",
    "NYPD collision reports and DOT's official Vision Zero count are compiled differently; DOT reviews each death before counting it, and its release does not spell out the criteria, so the gap between the two cannot be broken down here.",
    "2012 is left out because the collision dataset starts in July 2012.",
    "Peers use NHTSA FARS 2023, the latest final FARS year (NHTSA's 2024 file, released April 2026, is the annual report file, not yet final). FARS counts deaths within 30 days of a crash on a public road. For New York City FARS records 231 deaths in 2023, versus 280 in the NYPD collision data.",
    "Peers are counties, not cities: FARS city codes are inconsistent (much of Queens is coded under neighborhood names, and Nashville under Antioch, Madison and others), so each city's principal county is used, with the five counties for New York. Suburban and highway mileage in large counties such as Harris, Maricopa or Los Angeles differs from the city itself."
  ]
